          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    These are entry figures, not median outcomes for every graduate, and the upper end usually needs proof of skill, not the degree alone. Check live postings in your city before deciding.
  </p>

  <p>
    A wider backdrop helps. India's higher-education survey for 2021-22 showed 4.33 crore students, with science at 14.8% of undergraduate enrolment against 34.2% for arts.
    Science is a large group, so the entry-level crowd is large too. PLFS 2025 put youth (15-29) unemployment at 9.9%. That is why a visible skill matters more than the degree label.
  </p>

  <h2 id="weekly-reality">What the first job actually feels like</h2>

  <div class="reality-grid">
    <div class="reality-card">
      <div class="reality-card__title">QC or lab analyst</div>
      <p>Most of the week is sampling, running the same test, filling records and closing deviations. Precision and audit-ready documentation matter more than brilliance. Shifts are common.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="reality-card">
      <div class="reality-card__title">Junior data or reporting analyst</div>
      <p>Cleaning spreadsheets, writing SQL queries and preparing weekly reports. Explaining a number clearly to a manager is the skill that gets promotions.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="reality-card">
      <div class="reality-card__title">School or online teacher</div>
      <p>Lesson planning, marking and parent communication take more hours than the classroom. The reward is a predictable schedule and, for good teachers, a path to tuition income.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="reality-card">
      <div class="reality-card__title">Government exam aspirant</div>
      <p>Long, repeated study days and years of waiting, with little income and rising family pressure. It needs a clear attempt limit, and a side skill that survives an unsuccessful attempt.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money-runway">Should you spend on an MSc? The money math</h2>

  <p>
    A degree is not a complete career system. Tools, internet, books, projects and future learning also cost money. As a conservative starting heuristic, not a law,
    keep spending on the degree itself near <strong>10% of your total education budget</strong>. If your family's total education budget is ₹10 lakh, that points to about ₹1 lakh for the degree, with the rest kept for living
    security and skill-building.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Seats at public institutes through JAM or CUET PG are the strongest way to stay near that benchmark.</li>
    <li>Spending well above 20% on a private MSc or MBA needs course-specific evidence: verified placement median, placement denominator, faculty, labs and alumni outcomes.</li>
    <li>Avoid an education loan unless the credential is required for your target role or the programme has clear, verified outcomes.</li>
    <li>For a role that can be learned and shown without a degree, such as data analysis, compare the degree with strong YouTube learning, reputable online courses and real projects first, and judge each course by its syllabus, update date and the work it makes you produce.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The exceptions are real. Research careers, teaching posts and regulated roles need the formal credential, and internet learning does not replace it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-effect">What AI changes for BSc graduates</h2>

  <p>
    AI now drafts lab reports, cleans simple datasets and summarises papers. That pressure lands on the most routine entry tasks: formatting, first-pass analysis and template reports.
    Judgment in the lab, audit-ready documentation, field work and explaining results to non-experts remain human work.
  </p>

  <div class="stage-list">
    <div class="stage"><div class="stage__num">1</div><div><div class="stage__title">Now</div><p>Use an AI tool to draft, then check every number and source yourself. Learn spreadsheets and basic SQL in your own subject.</p></div></div>
    <div class="stage"><div class="stage__num">2</div><div><div class="stage__title">Next</div><p>Automate one repeated task from your lab or coursework, such as a report template or a data-cleaning routine, and keep a note of the time saved.</p></div></div>
    <div class="stage"><div class="stage__num">3</div><div><div class="stage__title">Later</div><p>Combine subject knowledge with workflow design and verification. This combination is harder to automate and supports a higher income ceiling, though no outcome is guaranteed.</p></div></div>
  </div>

  <p>
    No fixed multiplier is guaranteed. The gain comes from applying AI to a real problem in your subject with visible results, not from collecting tools.
  </p>

